#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Build the downloadable less-sheet artifacts, verify each one RUNS, and checksum them.

    ./make_release                        # everything this host can build
    ./make_release --platform mac
    ./make_release --platform linux --arch aarch64
    ./make_release --out /tmp/dist --version 0.1.0

PREPARES artifacts only. It never uploads, publishes, notarizes, or submits
anything, and it never signs with a Developer ID unless you explicitly export
LESSSHEET_CODESIGN_IDENTITY yourself. Publishing is a separate, deliberate act
(docs/RELEASE.md).

What it produces in --out (default <repo>/dist):

    less-sheet-<ver>-macos-arm64.zip        the .app, ditto-zipped (Apple's format)
    less-sheet-<ver>-macos-arm64.tar.gz     the same .app, for the curl one-liner
                                            (curl sets no quarantine bit, so it
                                             opens with no Gatekeeper prompt)
    less-sheet-<ver>-macos-arm64.dmg        the same .app in a plain disk image
    less-sheet-<ver>-linux-<arch>.tar.gz    GTK binary + .desktop + icon + install.sh
    SHA256SUMS                              one line per artifact
    manifest.json                           per-artifact runtime requirements + verdicts

Verification is by RUNNING, never by existence:
  * macOS - launches the PACKAGED app (unpacked from the artifact, not the build
    tree) headlessly via the LESSSHEET_* probe harness and requires the real
    open marker `lesssheet.first_rows_visible_ms=`, which the app only emits
    once a data-bearing table is on screen. No screen capture, so no TCC prompt.
  * Linux - unpacks the tarball inside a CLEAN fedora:43 container that has only
    the app's declared RUNTIME deps (gtk4, libadwaita - no compiler, no -devel,
    no meson) and requires both [timing] lines. This is the load-bearing test:
    it proves the artifact works on a machine that never built it.

Host requirements: zig 0.16.0 (exact, cross-builds the core). macOS artifacts
need a Mac with the Swift 6 toolchain. Linux artifacts need podman/docker; the
x86_64 leg runs under emulation on Apple Silicon and is slow but real.
"""

